This aircraft is a JSBSim model of an unmanned version of NASA's Quiet Single Main Rotor helicopter (QSMR, the
450 ft/s design), written by PteroLabs in 2026 and named Orca, with meshes built from NASA's OpenVSP model.

The flight model -- orca.xml, the files it assembles, Engines/ and Systems/ -- is PteroLabs' work, distributed under
the GNU General Public License v3.0; see LICENSE in this folder for its text. Its numbers come from:

  Johnson, "A Quiet Helicopter for Air Taxi Operations", VFS Aeromechanics for Advanced Vertical Flight Technical
  Meeting, 2020 (NTRS 20200000509), Table 2 and pp. 5-8: weights, rotor radius, blade count, solidity, twist, hinge
  offset, engine power and sfc, drag area, fuel tank, the anti-torque shares, and the performance the model is
  checked against.
    https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20200000509

  Ventura Diaz et al., VFS Forum 2022 (NTRS 20220006620), Tables 1, 3 and 4: rotor speed, blade chords, hover
  condition.
    https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20220006620

  NASA's OpenVSP model of the QSMR (meshes/NOTICE): all geometry and the projected areas measured on it.

  Whiteside et al., NASA/TM-20210017971: fixed useful load, the 6000 ft hover and 10000 ft cruise conditions.
    https://ntrs.nasa.gov/citations/20210017971

  NDARC Theory (2022) and Input (2025) manuals: lift-curve slope, inflow gradient, tail stall model, download
  factor, gearbox loss, engine rating ratios, ground effect.

  Logan, Morger and Sampatacos, "NOTAR demonstrator", 9th European Rotorcraft Forum (1983), paper 67: constant boom
  pressure, jet-to-wake speed ratio, fan efficiency, the direct-jet thruster's sleeve valve and its pedal schedule.

  Bakir, METU MSc thesis (2008), section 2.3.4 and chapter 3: the thruster's cones and openings, its momentum force,
  the slot momentum coefficient, the boom's fade with speed (Dawson and Thompson's data).

  Mechanika (2018), Table 2 (Sampatacos' experiment): the boom's side-force coefficient against the slot momentum
  coefficient; Luo, ICAS 2000: its saturation.

  Leishman, "Principles of Helicopter Aerodynamics", 2nd ed. (2006), chapters 3 and 10, and Landgrebe, JAHS 17(4)
  (1972): the hover inflow over the disc and the wake's contraction at the boom.

Chosen, with no source, and marked so where they appear: the blades' share of the rotor group's mass, the hub's
radius of gyration, the fan's mass and speed, the CG on the shaft line, the gear springs, damping and friction, the
fin and stabiliser maximum lift, the thruster's cone sizes and opening angles, the engine output speed, the governor
gains, the slot's segment count. README.md lists the model's known limits.

NOTAR is a registered trademark (US Reg. No. 1732062) of its owner. The anti-torque system here is a model of the
published principle, a circulation-control tail boom with a direct-jet thruster, not of any product.

